  "diagnosis_text": "The slowdown is measurable: h1\u2192h5 delivered about 0.20 Mbit/s, and ping RTT averaged 215 ms. The topology path is h1\u2013s4\u2013s2\u2013s1\u2013s3\u2013s6\u2013h5. So far, s2\u2019s port toward s1 shows queued traffic and repeated queue overlimit events; I\u2019ll check the remaining path and compare transfers that avoid that link.\n**The bottleneck is localised to s2 port 1, transmitting toward s1 port 1.** Evidence strongly suggests egress rate limiting/shaping at roughly **1 Mbit/s**, with queueing.\n\nThe h1\u2192h5 path is:\n`h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5`\n\nKey measurements:\n- **h1\u2192h5 TCP:** 0.20 Mbit/s received over a 5-second test.\n- **Ping:** 215 ms average RTT, 93\u2013374 ms range; no loss in five probes.\n- **s2\u2192s1:** two separate samples showed approximately 1.00 Mbit/s transmitted, persistent queue backlog (5\u201317 packets), and **355\u2013378 queue overlimit events per two seconds**.\n- Other sampled forward-path ports and the reverse-facing s1 port had no backlog or overlimit events. No sampled port reported drops or errors.\n- Transfers avoiding this link were fast: **h1\u2192h3 \u224833.8 Gbit/s** and **h7\u2192h5 \u224832.7 Gbit/s** in this virtual network, arguing against either endpoint or its local branch being the bottleneck.\n\n**Next step:** inspect the Linux traffic-control/qdisc and any OVS QoS configuration on **`s2-eth1`**, especially its rate, burst and queue limits. Traffic continued at about 1 Mbit/s outside my throughput test, so inspect competing traffic too; its identity was not measured.\n\nThe exact configured rate is not exposed by these tools, but the repeated rate plateau plus backlog and overlimit events strongly supports a shaping bottleneck rather than a link outage.",
